# Xbox editions (046d:c26d G923, 046d:c275 RS50): switch from console/boot
# mode to PC mode (046d:c26e, 046d:c276) on every plug-in.
#
# The Xbox edition of either wheel always boots into a console-only USB
# mode. In that mode it has no HID++ interface at all, so it shows up on
# `lsusb` but creates no /dev/input node - neither this driver nor the
# in-tree hid-logitech-hidpp can do anything with it. A single USB control
# message switches it into PC mode, which is the mode the rest of these
# rules and the driver both already handle. Neither wheel remembers the
# switch, so this has to fire on every single plug-in and cold boot, not
# just once.
#
# The switch command and its magic message are the same ones the G920
# family (046d:c261 console mode -> 046d:c262 PC mode) has used for
# years: message "0f00010142" to endpoint 1, expecting a response on
# endpoint 0x81, confirmed by the target settling on device class 0x03
# (HID). Sourced from batocera's udev/99-wheels.rules and the community
# four43/logitech-g923-driver install script, both of which apply this
# exact recipe to the G923 Xbox edition specifically. The RS50 pair
# (c275 -> c276) was confirmed on hardware by the reporter of issue #65,
# who ran the same message by hand and watched the wheel come back as
# "RS50 Base for PC".
#
# NEVER call usb_modeswitch from RUN+= directly. RUN+= executes inside
# the udev worker and holds the device lock for the whole USB control
# transfer. On a Legion Go running SteamOS that wedged USB hotplug badly
# enough to take the desktop down, and the machine would not finish
# booting while the wheel was attached; because the built-in controllers
# are internal USB devices, a stalled USB stack reads as a total freeze.
# No oops was recorded, which is what a lock-up rather than a crash looks
# like (issue #52, with thanks for a very precise diagnosis).
#
# So the work is dispatched with systemd-run --no-block and the worker
# returns immediately. This mirrors upstream usb_modeswitch, which ships
# usb_modeswitch@.service for exactly this reason.
#
# Driver contention: in console mode the wheel speaks Xbox GIP, so
# xbox-gip binds it first (the out-of-tree xone driver does the same on
# other setups), and usb_modeswitch cannot drive an interface another
# driver already owns. logi-wheel-modeswitch releases the holder before
# switching, so this is handled rather than merely documented.
#
# Without systemd-run this rule does nothing at all, deliberately: a
# silent no-op is a far better failure than freezing the machine. Run
# `sudo logi-wheel-modeswitch` by hand on such a system.
#
# Gated on usb_modeswitch actually being installed (it is a recommended,
# not hard, dependency in every packaging channel): distros disagree about
# whether it belongs in the system binary directory or the superuser one,
# the same file via a symlink on usrmerge systems and a genuinely separate
# package path otherwise, so `command -v` finds it wherever it lives
# instead of us hardcoding either. Without it this rule is a silent no-op.
#
# (Written without literal paths on purpose: NixOS validates udev rules at
# build time and treats any path-shaped string as a reference to check,
# comments included, so naming the directories here fails that build.)

ACTION!="add|change", GOTO="xbox_modeswitch_end"
SUBSYSTEM!="usb", GOTO="xbox_modeswitch_end"

ATTR{idVendor}=="046d", ATTR{idProduct}=="c26d|c275", PROGRAM=="/bin/sh -c 'command -v systemd-run >/dev/null && command -v usb_modeswitch >/dev/null && [ -x /usr/bin/logi-wheel-modeswitch ]'", RUN+="/bin/sh -c 'systemd-run --no-block --collect --description=\"Logitech wheel Xbox mode switch\" /usr/bin/logi-wheel-modeswitch >/dev/null 2>&1'"

LABEL="xbox_modeswitch_end"
